[2025-04-14 22:05:18,223][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "[0x9][0x9]<category><![CDATA[Rapid7]]></category>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,223][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "[0x9][0x9]<guid isPermaLink="false">https://krebsonsecurit"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,223][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "y.com/?p=70681</guid>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,223][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,223][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "[0x9][0x9][0x9][0x9][0x9]<description><![CDATA[Microsoft today issued more than 50 security updates for its various Windows operating systems, including fixes for a whopping six zero-day vulnerabilities that are already seeing active exploitation.]]></description>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,223][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "[0x9][0x9][0x9][0x9][0x9][0x9][0x9][0x9][0x9][0x9]<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Microsoft</strong> today issued more than 50 security updates for its various <strong>Windows</strong> operating systems, including fixes for a whopping <em>six zero-day vulnerabilities</em> that are already seeing active exploitation.</p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,223][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "<p><img decoding="async" loading="lazy" class="aligncenter wp-image-60331" src="https://krebsonsecurity.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/winupdatedate.png" alt="" width="749" height="496" srcset="https://krebsonsecurity.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/winupdatedate.png 923w, https://krebsonsecurity.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/winupdatedate-768x508.png 768w, https://krebsonsecurity.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/winupdatedate-782x518.png 782w" sizes="(max-width: 749px) 100vw, 749px" /></p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,223][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "<p>Two of the zero-day flaws include <a href="https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/en-US/advisory/CVE-2025-24991" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CVE-2025-24991</a> and <a href="https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/en-US/advisory/CVE-2025-24993" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-saferedirecturl="https://www.google.com/ur"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,223][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "l?q=https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/en-US/advisory/CVE-2025-24993&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1741811978241000&amp;usg=AOvVaw08YeX5TKKSxWlXHXx6FFHl">CVE-2025-24993</a>, both vulnerabilities in <strong>NTFS</strong>, the default file system for Windows and Windows Server. Both require the attacker to trick a target into mounting a malicious virtual hard disk. CVE-2025-24993 would lead to the possibility of local code execution, while CVE-2025-24991 could cause NTFS to disclose portions of memory.</p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,223][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "<p>Microsoft credits researchers at <strong>ESET</strong> with reporting the zero-day bug labeled <a href="https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/en-US/advisory/CVE-2025-24983" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CVE-2025-24983</a>, an elevation of privilege vulnerability in older versions of Windows. ESET said the exploit was deployed via the <a href="https://www.kaspersky.com/about/press-releases/kaspersky-uncovers-pipemagic-backdoor-attacks-businesses-through-fake-chatgpt-application" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PipeMagic backdoor</a>, capable of exfiltrating data and enabling remote access to the machine.</p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,223][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "<p>ESET&#8217;s <strong>Filip</strong> <b>Jur[0xc4][0x8d]acko </b>said the exploit in the wild targets only older versions of Windows OS: Windows 8.1 and Server 2012 R2. Although still used by millions, security support for these products ended more than a y"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,223][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "ear ago, and mainstream support ended years ago. However, ESET notes the vulnerability itself also is present in newer Windows OS versions, including <strong>Windows 10 build 1809</strong> and the still-supported <strong>Windows Server 2016</strong>.</p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,223][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "<p>Rapid7&#8217;s lead software engineer <strong>Adam Barnett</strong> said <strong>Windows 11</strong> and <strong>Server 2019</strong> onwards are not listed as receiving patches, so are presumably not vulnerable.</p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,224][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "<p>&#8220;It[0xe2][0x80][0x99]s not clear why newer Windows products dodged this particular bullet,&#8221; Barnett wrote. &#8220;The Windows 32 subsystem is still presumably alive and well, since there is no apparent mention of its demise on the Windows client OS deprecated features list.&#8221;<span id="more-70681"></span></p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,224][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "<p>The zero-day flaw <a href="https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/en-US/advisory/CVE-2025-24984" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CVE-2025-24984</a> is another NTFS weakness that can be exploited by inserting a malicious USB drive into a Windows computer. Barnett said Microsoft&#8217;s advisory for this bug doesn&#8217;t quite join the dots, but successful exploitation appears to mean that portions of heap memory could be improperly dumped into a log file, which could then be combed through by an attacker hungry for privileged information.</p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,224][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "<p>&#8220;A relatively low CVSSv3 ba"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,224][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "se score of 4.6 reflects the practical difficulties of real-world exploitation, but a motivated attacker can sometimes achieve extraordinary results starting from the smallest of toeholds, and Microsoft does rate this vulnerability as important on its own proprietary severity ranking scale,&#8221; Barnett said.</p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,224][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "<p>Another zero-day fixed this month &#8212; <a href="https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/en-US/advisory/CVE-2025-24985" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CVE-2025-24985</a> &#8212; could allow attackers to install malicious code. As with the NTFS bugs, this one requires that the user mount a malicious virtual hard drive.</p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,224][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "<p>The final zero-day this month is <a href="https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/en-US/advisory/CVE-2025-26633" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CVE-2025-26633</a>, a weakness in the <strong>Microsoft Management Console</strong>, a component of Windows that gives system administrators a way to configure and monitor the system. Exploiting this flaw requires the target to open a malicious file.</p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,224][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "<p>This month&#8217;s bundle of patch love from Redmond also addresses six other vulnerabilities Microsoft has rated &#8220;critical,&#8221; meaning that malware or malcontents could exploit them to seize control over vulnerable PCs with no help from users.</p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,224][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "<p>Barnett observed that this is now the sixth consecutive month whe"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,224][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "re Microsoft has published zero-day vulnerabilities on Patch Tuesday without evaluating any of them as critical severity at time of publication.</p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,224][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "<p>The[0xc2][0xa0]<a href="https://isc.sans.edu/forums/diary/Microsoft%20Patch%20Tuesday%3A%20March%202025/31756/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SANS Internet Storm Center</a> has a useful list of all the Microsoft patches released today, indexed by severity. Windows enterprise administrators would do well to keep an eye on <a href="http://www.askwoody.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">askwoody.com</a>, which often has the scoop on any patches causing problems. Please consider backing up your data before updating, and leave a comment below if you experience any issues applying this month&#8217;s updates.</p>[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:18,224][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-24 << "]]></content:encoded>[\n]"
